# PHY-007 — propagation-derived geometry comparison

**Scientific status: PASS_IN_SCOPE_CONDITIONAL_GEOMETRY. Emergent physical space is NOT derived.**

## Main result

For the local read-radius-one event class established in PHY-005/006, the shortest supported-path distance has an operational meaning that does not require an embedding: it is exactly the minimum event-layer depth at which influence can possibly become nonzero, provided each declared support edge has nonzero conditional influence.

Thus the first admitted geometry candidate is not Euclidean distance but **causal-depth distance on the supplied support graph**.

## Metric controls

Four frozen 12-cell controls were tested: a path, a ring, one fixed 3-regular random graph (seed 17) as an expander-like bounded-degree control, and dense `K12`.

All three graph distances tested were valid metrics numerically on the connected controls:

- hop/shortest-support distance;
- effective resistance from the positive weighted Laplacian, normalized by the median edge resistance;
- diffusion distance at declared dimensionless time `t=1`, normalized by the median edge distance.

Fisher-Rao distance was checked separately as a metric on probability states. It is a **state-space control**, not a cell-space geometry, because it contains no cell adjacency.

## Agreement with causal depth

Hop distance agrees exactly by theorem. Spearman correlations of the other graph metrics with causal depth were:

| graph | resistance | diffusion t=1 |
|---|---:|---:|
| path12 | 0.9927 | 0.9022 |
| ring12 | 0.9851 | 0.9884 |
| 3-regular control | 0.9221 | 0.9308 |
| dense K12 | non-informative | non-informative |

The dense control has graph diameter one, so no graded notion of local separation survives.

## Representation and model sensitivity

Node relabeling only permutes every distance matrix. Common positive scaling of edge conductances leaves hop distance exactly invariant and, after the declared normalizations, leaves resistance and random-walk diffusion invariant to floating precision.

Relative edge reweighting is not a coordinate change; it changes the physical conditional model. Under one frozen 0.7–1.3 reweighting, resistance and diffusion distances change at the several-percent level while hop support distance remains unchanged. This is expected: resistance/diffusion encode weighted transport details, while hop distance encodes the exact discrete causal cone.

A common orthogonal change of the seven-dimensional feature frame preserves the mean-difference edge cost and does not change graph support.

## Decision

A conditional **causal-depth geometry** survives the PHY-007 gates without threshold tuning. It is useful for refinement and influence tests. It is not yet emergent space because the graph support remains a PM input. Resistance and diffusion are legitimate secondary geometries once weights/generator are supplied, but FIN does not currently select them uniquely.
